Writing a generic class.

Consuming a list of T is one thing; writing your own type with a parameter is where generics really click. A generic class declares a type parameter after its name, and then uses that parameter throughout — as a field type, a method argument, a return type — as if it were a real type that it just doesn't know yet.

You declare the parameter in angle brackets, right after the class name. Take a box class, box of T. Inside, it has a private field, value, of the unknown type T. Its constructor takes a T. Its get method returns a T. And its set method takes a T. From there, at the use site, the caller picks T, and every T locks to it. So a box of string: you get a string back with no cast, because T is string here. And if you try to set forty-two, that's a compile error, because T is string, not an int. Box is written once, works for any type, and each use is fully checked. This is exactly how the collection classes are built.

You can declare several type parameters, comma-separated — which is how map, of K and V, works, and how a pair works. A pair record of A and B, holding a first and a second. So a pair of string and Integer: first gives you the string, second gives you the integer. And a generic record is often the cleanest way to return two values from a method, without inventing a one-off class.

Now, three rules that surprise people, because a type parameter is a compile-time placeholder, and — for reasons the erasure section will make clear — it isn't a fully real type at run time. First, you can't new a T. New T won't compile, because the class doesn't know which constructor to call. The workaround is to pass in a factory — a supplier of T, or a T value. Second, you can't have a static member of type T. T belongs to an instance — a box of string versus a box of Integer — so a static field of type T is meaningless, and rejected. A static method can still be generic, but with its own parameter; that's section four. And third, you can't new a T array. Generic array creation is a compile error, for reasons in section nine; instead, you back a generic container with an Object array and cast internally, exactly as array-list itself does.

One last thing, a first taste of bounds. Inside box of T, T could be any type, so you can only call Object methods on it, like to-string and equals. The moment you need more — say, to compare or measure T — you bound it, promising that T has certain capabilities. A sorted box, of T extends comparable, means T must be comparable, so now T has a compare-to method. That extends is a constraint, not inheritance — and it's the subject of section five.

So the takeaway: a generic class is an ordinary class, with one or more type placeholders. Declare them after the name, use them as real types inside, and remember the three "you can't" rules that fall out of how generics actually run. Next: making an individual method generic, independent of its class.
